The gradient at the specified point is normal to the level curve. Differentiate before substituting the coordinates.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_x(x,y)&=1 - 2 x \\ f_y(x,y)&=- 4 y \\ \nabla f(1,1)&=\left(-1\right)\,i+\left(-4\right)\,j \\ \|\nabla f(1,1)\|&=\sqrt{\left(-1\right)^2+\left(-4\right)^2} \\ &=\sqrt{17}
\end{aligned}
$$

This gradient is nonzero. Divide it by its magnitude:

$$
\begin{aligned}\mathbf n&=\frac{\nabla f(1,1)}{\|\nabla f(1,1)\|} \\ &=\frac{\left(-1\right)\,i+\left(-4\right)\,j}{\sqrt{17}} \\ &=\left(- \frac{\sqrt{17}}{17}\right)\,i+\left(- \frac{4 \sqrt{17}}{17}\right)\,j
\end{aligned}
$$

Its negative is also a unit normal. Exactly one displayed choice is a unit vector parallel to the gradient.
